  "query_preview": "..The main weather story for this week will \ncontinue to be the heat with an extended stretch of near record to \nrecord high temperatures starting Tuesday. This is all courtesy of \nan anomalously strong area of high pressure aloft. This high is \ncurrently centered over New Mexico and will briefly weaken on Monday \nfor some slight 'cooling', but that will be short lived as the high \nre-strenthens starting Tuesday and shifts west into Arizona \nWednesday, continuing into the weekend. The high pressure aloft will \npeak in intensity mid to late week with 500mb heights up to around 3 \nstandard deviations above normal. The Extreme Heat Warning is in \neffect for much of southeast Arizona through Saturday.  \n\nOutside of the heat concerns, we've still got some monsoonal \nmoisture across our area. Latest IR satellite imagery shows \ncontinued southeast flow across our forecast area with clear to \npartly cloudy skies. Our focus for storms today will once again \nmostly be on locales from Tucson south and westward (where the \ngreatest instability is) as drier air remains closer to the NM \nborder. Main concern with these storms will continue to be wind with \na secondary isolated threat for heavy rain/flash flooding. Included \nsome patchy blowing dust into the forecast later this afternoon into \nthe evening from the T.O. Nation north into Pinal County with some \ngusty outflow winds there. A very similar setup for storm activity \nis expected on Monday before a more notable downward trend in \nconvection starting Tuesday and continuing through the rest of the \nwork week with storm chances mostly hugging right along the Int'l \nborder and White Mountains. Generally little if any activity \nelsewhere as subsidence and drier air aloft limits storm chances. \nThere are some hints of an uptick in convection next weekend as \nmoisture increases and the high might shift just enough for more \nfavorable northeast flow.",
